  "reveal": {
    "afterSuccess": {
      "data": "However you grouped the faithful steps — all three separately, or with a pre-bundle — you got the same true report. That's associativity: f then (g then h) equals (f then g) then h. The buggy bundles had the right types but the wrong behavior, so they fell out.",
      "alchemy": "However you grouped the faithful steps, you reached the same true alloy. That's associativity: regrouping never changes the result. The botched bundles had the right types but wrong behavior.",
      "spellcraft": "However you grouped the faithful steps, you reached the same true ward. That's associativity: regrouping never changes the result. The broken bundles had the right types but wrong behavior.",
      "abstract": "Every faithful grouping produced D's value: h ∘ (g ∘ f) = (h ∘ g) ∘ f. Associativity lets you bundle freely; only behavior-correct steps qualify."
    },
    "summary": "Associativity: h ∘ (g ∘ f) = (h ∘ g) ∘ f. You can compose a chain in any grouping and get the same morphism — so a pre-bundled sub-step is interchangeable with doing its steps one by one.",
    "notation": ["h ∘ (g ∘ f) = (h ∘ g) ∘ f"],
    "programmerAnalogy": "h(g(f(x))) — where you put the parentheses doesn't change the answer."
  },
